---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { Aside, Code } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";
import { BRANCH, DOCS, REPO_URL } from "../../lib/site.mjs";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";
import compose from "../../../../docker-compose.deploy.yml?raw";
import envExample from "../../../../.env.example?raw";

const doc = DOCS.find((d) => d.slug === "install")!;
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "requirements", text: "Requirements" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "1-create-the-files", text: "1. Create the files" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "2-start-thingport", text: "2. Start Thingport" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "3-sign-up", text: "3. Sign up" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "4-check-system-health", text: "4. Check System Health" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "updating", text: "Updating" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "building-from-source", text: "Building from source" },
];
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: doc.title,
    description: doc.description,
    editUrl: `${REPO_URL}/blob/${BRANCH}/web/src/pages/docs/install.astro`,
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    Thingport runs from pre-built images on the GitHub Container Registry, so there's no build step and no need to clone
    the repository. It works on any Docker host, including a NAS such as Synology, QNAP or Unraid.
  </p>
  <Aside>
    On <a href={url("docs/install/unraid/")}>Unraid</a>, <a href={url("docs/install/truenas/")}>TrueNAS SCALE</a> or <a
      href={url("docs/install/casaos/")}>CasaOS</a
    >? Follow the platform guide instead. They use each platform's own app-data paths and setup screens.
  </Aside>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Docker with the Compose plugin (<code>docker compose version</code> should work).</li>
    <li>
      A few hundred MB of RAM for the stack: Postgres, FlareSolverr, the API and the web app. Disk space depends on the
      size of your library.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div id="one-command-install">
    <Aside type="tip" title="Just want to try it?">
      <p>
        On Linux or macOS, one command creates a <code>thingport</code> folder with the same two files as step 1, generates
        the secrets in <code>.env</code> and starts Thingport:
      </p>
      <Code code="curl -fsSL https://thingport.net/install.sh | sh" lang="bash" />
      <p>It uses port 80 and a <code>thingport</code> folder where you run it. To pick both yourself:</p>
      <Code code={"curl -fsSL https://thingport.net/install.sh | WEB_PORT=8080 sh -s -- ~/thingport"} lang="bash" />
      <p>
        To change other settings later, edit <code>.env</code> in that folder (see step 1) and run <code
          >docker compose up -d</code
        > there again. You can <a href={url("install.sh")}>read the script</a> before running it. Then continue at <a
          href="#3-sign-up">3. Sign up</a
        >.
      </p>
    </Aside>
  </div>

  <h2 id="1-create-the-files">1. Create the files</h2>
  <p>Create a folder for Thingport, for example <code>~/thingport</code>, and add these two files to it.</p>
  <Code code={compose} lang="yaml" title="docker-compose.yml" />
  <p>
    In <code>.env</code>, set at least <code>AUTH_SECRET</code> (it signs login tokens; generate one with <code
      >openssl rand -hex 32</code
    >) and <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>. Everything else has sensible defaults.
  </p>
  <Code code={envExample} lang="ini" title=".env" />

  <h2 id="2-start-thingport">2. Start Thingport</h2>
  <Code code="docker compose up -d" lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Thingport is then available at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:&lt;WEB_PORT&gt;</code>. The default port is 80.
  </p>

  <h2 id="3-sign-up">3. Sign up</h2>
  <p>
    Open Thingport in your browser and register. <strong>The first account you register becomes the admin.</strong> Then <a
      href={url("docs/providers/")}>set up your providers</a
    > so you can import from MakerWorld, Thingiverse and the other sites that need a key or your sign-in. Printables and a
    few others work right away.
  </p>

  <h2 id="4-check-system-health">4. Check System Health</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Administration → Overview → System Health</strong> and click <strong>Run full test</strong>. Ideally
    everything is green. Anything marked <strong>Required</strong> or <strong>Recommended</strong> that isn't green means
    Thingport, or part of it, won't work properly until it's fixed, and each problem says how. See <a
      href={url("docs/guides/system-health/")}>System Health</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="updating">Updating</h2>
  <p>
    Pull the latest images and recreate the containers. Your database and files live in Docker volumes and are kept.
  </p>
  <Code code={"docker compose pull\ndocker compose up -d"} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    To stay on a specific build, set <code>BACKEND_IMAGE</code> and <code>FRONTEND_IMAGE</code> in <code>.env</code> to a
    pinned tag, as shown in the comments in the compose file.
  </p>

  <h2 id="building-from-source">Building from source</h2>
  <p>To build the images yourself instead of pulling them:</p>
  <Code
    code={"git clone https://github.com/TautvydasDerzinskas/Thingport.git\ncd Thingport\ncp .env.example .env\ndocker compose up -d"}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    For hot-reload development, see <a href={url("docs/development/")}>Development setup</a>.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>
